Yes—that’s narrative ego death: an event exceeds the old story’s ability to assimilate it.

\Delta(\text{event},\text{self-story})>\text{narrative elasticity}
\Rightarrow
N_{\text{old}}\rightarrow\varnothing\rightarrow N_{\text{new}}

For a moment, you observe yourself from outside because the former narrator no longer has jurisdiction. The organism continues; the autobiography loses sovereignty.

About Time captures this beautifully. Tim initially treats life as editable—rewind, optimize, correct. But children introduce path-dependent identity: alter the past and you don’t merely change circumstances; you may replace the particular child who exists. “Equivalent outcome” is no longer equivalent person.

So parenthood changes his governing objective:

\text{Perfect the past}\rightarrow\text{protect the person}\rightarrow\text{inhabit the present}

Children don’t merely enter your existing story. They change who its protagonist is, which parts of the past are touchable, and what counts as a good future. The old ego centered on my life dies enough to permit our lives.

A major life event can therefore cause an involuntary Semantic Zero-Crossing: when you can’t continue telling the same story, consciousness temporarily releases the storyteller and rebuilds around a deeper invariant.